      ],
      "canonical_aliases": [],
      "source_authors": [
        "University of Birmingham"
      ],
      "description": "The University of Birmingham reports experiments in which active mechanical units settle into coordinated oscillation through local, non-reciprocal interactions. Initially short waves grow in scale, period, and amplitude until the material moves together. The report describes both a honeycomb assembly and an elastic ring, and calls the process wave coarsening. It provides a concrete physical example of coordination arising from designed interactions. Proposed uses in robotics remain prospective applications of the reported experiments.",
